WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.783
(Music)

00:00:08.784 --> 00:00:10.667
My name is Gary Feierabend.

00:00:10.668 --> 00:00:12.964
I’m the Head of Engineering Transformation in Canada

00:00:12.965 --> 00:00:16.657
and responsible for launching Manulife University globally.

00:00:16.658 --> 00:00:19.306
My area deals with the agile practices,

00:00:19.307 --> 00:00:22.447
the engineering cadre, and security engineering of how

00:00:22.448 --> 00:00:24.391
we do things in Manulife.

00:00:24.392 --> 00:00:27.157
My team is made up of several functions.

00:00:27.158 --> 00:00:29.537
The first part of it is Manulife University.

00:00:29.538 --> 00:00:31.612
Manulife University is really about what we call

00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:33.649
an upskilling and mastering program.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.747
At Manulife, in the beginning it was very difficult

00:00:36.748 --> 00:00:39.607
to maintain our ability to stay up to date on

00:00:39.608 --> 00:00:42.938
technologies, anything to adopt modern stacks.

00:00:42.939 --> 00:00:45.761
In order to establish programs working with Pluralsight,

00:00:45.762 --> 00:00:48.348
Manulife University for campus-based training or

00:00:48.349 --> 00:00:51.264
virtual-based training to really ramp up engineers

00:00:51.265 --> 00:00:55.659
of how we get things done is a critical aspect of what we do.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.612
But there’s another important side, and that is how we

00:00:58.613 --> 00:01:01.741
also demonstrate, can you apply those skills that

00:01:01.742 --> 00:01:04.712
you’ve learned back into the squad or back into the

00:01:04.713 --> 00:01:06.076
projects that you’re working on.

00:01:06.077 --> 00:01:08.653
One of the challenges I think that Pluralsight Flow

00:01:08.654 --> 00:01:12.995
really helped us to realize was prior to us introducing

00:01:12.996 --> 00:01:15.294
Flow in our organization, we just didn’t have enough

00:01:15.295 --> 00:01:18.343
data, so we were just really looking at burndown rates

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:20.681
and number of stories completed, but it didn’t give us

00:01:20.682 --> 00:01:24.370
the context of what our engineering teams were really focused on.

00:01:24.371 --> 00:01:27.224
We are customer obsessed. We find that the best way

00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:29.555
to be customer obsessed is to be obsessed about our

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:31.370
engineers and our employees.

00:01:31.371 --> 00:01:34.583
Since we’ve started leveraging Flow, we are able to start

00:01:34.584 --> 00:01:37.488
finding optimizations in terms of how frequently we

00:01:37.489 --> 00:01:40.096
are committing code, making sure that we’re doing

00:01:40.097 --> 00:01:43.153
more effective code reviews, and that all relates to

00:01:43.154 --> 00:01:44.952
better code quality.

00:01:44.953 --> 00:01:48.498
Flow data is power. Some of the major benefits that

00:01:48.499 --> 00:01:51.597
I got with Flow is to learn based on data.

00:01:51.598 --> 00:01:55.006
In my case, the sessions that I had with my manager

00:01:55.007 --> 00:01:58.664
were very insightful. I learned that maybe I should be

00:01:58.665 --> 00:02:01.318
taking more time on looking for opportunities to

00:02:01.319 --> 00:02:04.247
create new features rather than maybe looking at

00:02:04.248 --> 00:02:07.783
that legacy code. So I think by having these conversations,

00:02:07.784 --> 00:02:11.037
by looking at those metrics, we have an opportunity for

00:02:11.038 --> 00:02:13.967
not only knowing what is happening on our development

00:02:13.968 --> 00:02:17.950
cycles, but to learn and to enhance that development process.

00:02:17.951 --> 00:02:20.885
Skills from Pluralsight was a very valuable asset

00:02:20.886 --> 00:02:23.149
because we recognized people needed to learn at

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.680
their frequency, their times, but there was one much

00:02:26.681 --> 00:02:29.184
more important aspect to Skills.

00:02:29.185 --> 00:02:33.513
Role IQ and Skill IQ removes the bias that exists

00:02:33.514 --> 00:02:35.873
within organizations about who would be a good

00:02:35.874 --> 00:02:39.709
front-end engineer or data engineer or data scientist.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.947
It’s much more quantifiable data, and now we can

00:02:42.948 --> 00:02:45.329
use it in promotional material to get that person

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.320
to the next level of their career.

00:02:47.321 --> 00:02:48.707
We had one beautiful example.

00:02:48.708 --> 00:02:51.432
We found an individual that was at the associate

00:02:51.433 --> 00:02:54.990
level for five years, yet this associate level,

00:02:54.991 --> 00:02:57.397
through Skill IQ and again through Flow,

00:02:57.398 --> 00:02:59.952
helped identify how much they really get done on

00:02:59.953 --> 00:03:02.401
the squad, but they just don’t happen to be verbal

00:03:02.402 --> 00:03:06.235
about it, they don’t happen to stand up at the retro

00:03:06.236 --> 00:03:08.508
and really showcase what they’re able to do.

00:03:08.509 --> 00:03:11.439
Instead of releases that were once a quarter,

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.416
we’ve started releasing once a month,

00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:16.019
and then now releasing every sprint,

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.167
releasing features, new product evolutions

00:03:20.168 --> 00:03:23.862
because they gain skills, they’re transparent in terms

00:03:23.863 --> 00:03:26.096
of what’s happening within the squad and what

00:03:26.097 --> 00:03:27.877
are the blockers that they’re running into and

00:03:27.878 --> 00:03:31.473
what’s causing churn. It leads to a better product,

00:03:31.474 --> 00:03:34.578
and the product our customer sees.

